---
// /about — the narrative. The quietest page on the site: long-form, film
// ground, generous measure, one scroll device (a gold reading-progress
// hairline). No Build Log, no motifs, no accents beyond gold and one
// Redline mark. Byline: Dennis De Leon.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { breadcrumbs } from '../lib/jsonld';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — The Linova Group"
  description="Computer science, then cold calls, then a system: why Linova builds machine-fast and markets human-real. By Dennis De Leon, founder."
  jsonLd={[breadcrumbs([{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'About', path: '/about' }])]}
>
  <div class="about bg-film text-navy" data-ground="film">
    <!-- the one scroll device: a reading-progress hairline -->
    <div class="progress-track fixed inset-x-0 top-0 z-50 h-px bg-navy/10">
      <div class="progress-fill h-full w-full origin-left scale-x-0 bg-gold" data-progress></div>
    </div>

    <article class="mx-auto max-w-[680px] px-6 pb-28 pt-24">
      <header>
        <p class="label-mono !text-gold-deep">about · the linova group</p>
        <h1 class="mt-6 font-display text-title leading-[1.08]">
          I learned to build software, then I learned why people buy it.
          This company is what happened when the two finally met.
        </h1>
        <p class="label-mono mt-8 normal-case tracking-normal !text-gold-deep">
          Dennis De Leon · founder, Thornhill, Ontario
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="about-body mt-14 flex flex-col gap-6 text-body">
        <p>
          My background is computer science. My first real job was not writing
          code — it was B2B sales. Cold calls, BDR lists, hearing "no" forty
          times a day and learning what made the forty-first call different.
          That combination is rarer than it should be: most developers have
          never had to sell anything, and most salespeople can't build what
          they're selling. I ended up able to do both, and for a while I
          didn't know what that was for.
        </p>

        <p>
          Then I started paying attention to how small businesses around the
          GTA actually get websites. A restaurant, a landscaping crew, a
          taekwondo school, a transport company — they all hit the same two
          doors. Door one: an agency quotes them fifteen or twenty thousand
          dollars and a three-month timeline for what turns out to be a
          template with their logo on it. Door two: they pay a few hundred
          bucks for the template directly, and it costs them quietly, every
          week, in customers who looked at the site and called someone else.
          Both doors are bad. The owners know it. They just haven't had a
          third door.
        </p>

        <p>
          When AI code tools showed up, everyone assumed the third door was
          "let the machine write it." It isn't. I've seen what that produces
          — it's a genre now. Sites that demo beautifully and fall apart the
          first time a menu changes or a form fails or a page needs to exist
          in two places. The industry calls it vibe coding, and the name is
          honest: there's a vibe, and there's no engineering under it.
        </p>

        <p>
          What I built instead is a system. Every site starts as typed data —
          the menu, the services, the projects, the hours, all of it lives in
          structured modules with interfaces that enforce their shape.
          Components read the data; nothing is hand-pasted into a page. The
          build is deterministic: same inputs, same output, every time. AI
          works inside that discipline as an accelerant — it makes me faster
          at the parts that were already engineered, and it is never allowed
          to improvise the architecture. That's the entire difference. It's
          why one developer can ship an agency-grade site in days instead of
          months, and why the output survives contact with production —
          across dozens of client sites, not impressively once.
        </p>

        <p>
          That's half the company. Here's the other half, and I'm going to
          say it plainly because most people in my position won't:
          <strong class="font-medium">AI cannot do marketing.</strong>
          It can compress a build. It cannot read a neighbourhood. It can't
          feel out a brand's voice, or know why one Reel lands and the next
          one dies, or understand what a Filipino restaurant means to its
          community in a way that shows up in the caption. Marketing runs on
          judgment, cultural read, and taste — and those come from
          <span class="marker-u">people</span>.
        </p>

        <p>
          So Linova's marketing is done by a human team in the Philippines —
          real strategists and creatives who plan campaigns, write the words,
          and make the content. Not AI slop with a human's name on it. Their
          faces and names belong on this page, and they're coming to it as
          soon as the photos are in — that's a promise to them as much as to
          you.
        </p>

        <p>
          That's the whole company. Machine-fast where machines are genuinely
          better: the build, the data, the pipeline, the performance. Human
          where humans are genuinely better: the strategy, the words, the
          taste. Neither half pretending to be the other. If that sounds like
          the third door you've been looking for,
          <a href="/book" class="text-navy underline decoration-gold decoration-2 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-redline">book a call</a>
          — it's twenty minutes, and I'm the one who picks up.
        </p>
      </div>

      <footer class="mt-16 border-t border-navy/15 pt-8">
        <p class="m-0 font-display text-heading italic">— Dennis De Leon</p>
        <p class="label-mono mt-2 normal-case tracking-normal !text-gold-deep">
          founder &amp; lead developer
        </p>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about-body p {
    margin: 0;
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .progress-track {
      display: none;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  import { prefersReducedMotion } from '../lib/motion';

  const fill = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-progress]');
  if (fill && !prefersReducedMotion()) {
    const update = () => {
      const max = document.documentElement.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight;
      fill.style.transform = `scaleX(${max > 0 ? Math.min(1, window.scrollY / max) : 0})`;
    };
    document.addEventListener('scroll', update, { passive: true });
    update();
  }
</script>
